1 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503611 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503611 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503611 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503611 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186503037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503611 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c29dba39-a035-580c-9c90-3ddf062b8c8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503612 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503612 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503612 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503612 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503612 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503612 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503612 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503612 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186503342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503612 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c41454b8-a54a-5924-9bf1-be03ac5b4d5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503637 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503637 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503637 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503637 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503637 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503637 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503637 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503637 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186503514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503637 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f07b5cf1-c779-56d4-b26c-f37aa9c40d28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503654 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503654 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79412" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503654 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503654 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3c/3cff4fddd4ae530a233a4f667b3fc84cc2109914a59dda332cbcc8bd5838a516.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503654 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ea6bbbd-4b7d-5900-9086-5360a21a6636" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503654 #edb/ref 1074 "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function $y=g(x)$ has inflection points at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5.$ At the inflection points, we have ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5.$\n\nWe don't know the exact values of ${g}^{′}(-0.5)$ and ${g}^{′}(0.5)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$ we find that the slope of the tangents to ${g}^{′}(x)$ at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5$ are negative and positive, respectively. \n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/b5/b5cdb9170fbf8521bddc184d7a58cf0666ed9ad7d6323407ba1f518c59a9ede9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503658 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503658 #edb/ref 1069 "q-82180" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503658 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503658 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $f(x)$ that satisfies the conditions below?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ and ${f}^{″}(x)$ are undefined at $x=1$\n\n- if $x\\in (-\\infty ,1)$ then ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ and if $x\\in (1,\\infty )$ then ${f}^{′}(x)>0$\n\n- if $x\\in (-\\infty ,1)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ and if $x\\in (1,\\infty )$ then ${f}^{″}(x)>0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503658 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1508af35-4265-5067-b707-b4b8e57d8d0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503658 #edb/ref 1074 "We summarize all the given information in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘\\,\\,⌢$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗\\,\\,⌣$ |\n\nNow, let's sketch our graph: \n\n- We draw a point on the vertical line $x=1$, where the derivative is undefined.\n\n- Since $x=1$ is a local minimum, the graph must go up to the left and to the right of that point.\n\n- The graph must be concave down on $(-\\infty ,1)$ and concave up on $(1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Finally, we are told that the function is continuous.\n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ could be as follows:\n\n![](images/0b/0b08e02b0999da13ec71b2fee4e2df4a8754f54d99cb711e6ff19c7a81ace9de.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503663 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503663 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503663 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503663 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e7/e74f96531b57a877b043ac4302adae6beef90dc24d64efc8e1a24b563e4738b7.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the ellipse shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503663 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e5b95a8-8914-5efe-a3ab-4b5dce1c8cbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503663 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our ellipse is centered at $(-1,2)$ and has semiaxes $4$ and $2,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(x+1)}{16}+\\frac{2(y-2)}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+1}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+1}{4(y-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503669 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503669 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503669 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503669 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation $4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503669 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28cd517a-376e-5db1-863f-bc38045c2ab7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503669 #edb/ref 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above definition into the equation $4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})) & =-3 \\\\ 2({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) & =-3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{-x} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${e}^{x},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{-x}) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-3) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}-2 & =-3{e}^{x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{x}-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in ${e}^{x}$ that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{e}^{x}-1)({e}^{x}+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n2{e}^{x}-1=0,\\,{e}^{x}+2=0\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}-1 & =0 &  & \\, & {e}^{x}+2 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\frac{1}{2} &  & \\, & {e}^{x} & =-2 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) &  & \\, & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no real values of $x$ for which ${e}^{x}=-2.$ Therefore, $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})$ is the only real solution to the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503677 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503677 #edb/ref 1069 "q-149457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503677 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503677 #edb/ref 1071 "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ and its derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ over the closed interval $[1,9].$\nUsing a right Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals, estimate the arc length of the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=1$ and $x=9.$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $4$ | $5$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $4\\sqrt{5}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $3\\sqrt{7}$ |\n\n*Hint: The arc length of a smooth curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by*\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503677 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d6689df-3f70-5bf3-89ff-1a1c94f97ed3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503677 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the hint, we have that the arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=1$ and $x=9$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{9-1}{4}=2.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *right* Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{9}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈(g(3)+g(5)+g(7)+g(9))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nNow, we set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $4\\sqrt{5}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $3\\sqrt{7}$ |\n| $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}$ | $9$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our arc length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈(g(3)+g(5)+g(7)+g(9))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1+2+1+8)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503683 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503683 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503683 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503683 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$6.$ If the hypotenuse of the triangle lies along the diameter of the semicircle, what is the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503683 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ce61c51-3f39-5f7e-96d5-a322aa4a1799" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503683 #edb/ref 1074 "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/41/417b284c87f735eb0c33f2369262b1548f9c54ff27ecbeb7880e91e9bc847f31.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =12+l+\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $12,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<12.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}} & =l \\\\ 144-{l}^{2} & ={l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =144 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =72 \\\\ l & =6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=6\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,6\\sqrt{2})$ | $6\\sqrt{2}$ | $(6\\sqrt{2},12)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $9$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=6\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6\\sqrt{2}) & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{144-(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{144-72} \\\\  & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+6\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =12+12\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =12(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503687 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503687 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503687 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503687 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x}{x-1})}^{3x-3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503687 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55d1a1bd-c8eb-5ee7-88d6-a44ae7dc2c96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503687 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x}{x-1})}^{3x-3}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x}{x-1})}^{3x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(x-1)+1}{x-1})}^{3x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x-1})}^{3(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $x-1=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x}{x-1})}^{3x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x-1})}^{3(x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{3n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503709 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503709 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503709 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503709 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $g(x)=\\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{2x+1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503709 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "828244f0-37d1-5218-b4cd-65ea8c882592" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503709 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, the expression for $g(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={sinh}^{2}⁡x$ and $v(x)=2x+1.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({sinh}^{2}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (2x+1)-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot (2x+1)^{′}}{(2x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\cdot (2x+1)-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot (2)}{(2x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2x+1)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503711 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503711 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48021" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503711 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503711 #edb/ref 1071 "Approximate the value of $\\sqrt{37}$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ at $x=36.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503711 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "82a6bab7-1298-5d9a-9389-41a0c015118d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503711 #edb/ref 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$ is\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=36,$ which gives\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(36)+{f}^{′}(36)(x-36).\n$$\n\nLet's compute $f(36)$ and ${f}^{′}(36)$ in the expression above. Evaluating the function, we get\n\n$$\nf(36)=\\sqrt{36}=6,\n$$\n\nand computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(36)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{36}}=\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x)=6+\\frac{1}{12}(x-36).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\sqrt{37}$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=37.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{37} & ≈L(37) \\\\  & =6+\\frac{1}{12}(37-36) \\\\  & =6+\\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{73}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503759 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0341db66-4a35-52c0-ad9b-5f93a0cf7225" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503759 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186234323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503760 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "053d77c3-8a49-5705-b184-df8c4f01cb7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503760 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186503683 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
